<h1>pg_peek</h1>

<article aria-label="Authentication is not configured">
  <header>Authentication is not configured</header>
  <p>
    pg_peek exposes your schema, every normalized query in the database, and an
    endpoint that resets query statistics. It refuses to serve
    <code><%= Rails.env %></code> until you say how it should be protected.
  </p>

  <p>Either give it credentials, and it will ask for them:</p>
  <pre><code>PG_PEEK_USERNAME=... PG_PEEK_PASSWORD=...</code></pre>
  <p>or, equivalently, in <code>config/credentials.yml.enc</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>pg_peek:
  username: ...
  password: ...</code></pre>

  <p>
    Or, if the engine is already mounted behind your own authentication, say so
    in <code>config/initializers/pg_peek.rb</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>config.public_dashboard = true</code></pre>

  <footer>
    <small>
      Development and test are exempt, so this never interrupts local work.
      Credentials, once set, apply everywhere &mdash; including a development
      server exposed through a tunnel.
    </small>
  </footer>
</article>
